## Final recommendation: choose **TraceDesk**, subject to a focused two-week validation pilot

The deciding requirement is not general task management or ease of setup. It is the ability to manage **cross-company dependencies, contractual milestones, and vendor-specific access without repeatedly reconciling separate boards**.

The recent independent evaluation found that TraceDesk:

- exposed dependencies across companies;
- restricted each vendor to its own documents;
- preserved a shared milestone history.

Boardly, although easier and more familiar, required separate boards and manual status reconciliation for the same workflow. That creates a **recurring operational burden** and raises the chance that dependency changes or contractual dates will be missed. Over a year-long migration, that matters more than the one-time benefit of faster setup.

### The trade-off

TraceDesk has a steeper learning curve and higher external-collaborator costs. You are giving up Boardly’s comfortable interface, rapid adoption, and lower transition effort. Those are real costs, not incidental drawbacks.

Nevertheless, the implementation note indicates that TraceDesk’s workflow advantage became more noticeable over several weeks, while the initial demonstration understated it. That is especially relevant because your two-week pilot will use a small sample: it may overstate Boardly’s simplicity while failing to reveal the cumulative cost of reconciliation at full scale.

### How to use the pilot

Treat the pilot as a test of the recommendation, not simply as a vote on which interface feels better. Include:

1. at least two external vendors with different access permissions;
2. one milestone tied to a contractual date;
3. a dependency crossing vendor boundaries;
4. a change to that dependency or milestone;
5. an overdue item and an escalation;
6. a weekly status report or audit-history check.

Measure:

- time spent reconciling status;
- whether every dependency has an owner and current date;
- whether vendors see only authorized material;
- whether milestone changes remain traceable;
- time required to produce an accurate cross-vendor report;
- user training and support effort.

Set pass/fail thresholds before the pilot. Also model collaborator pricing at the expected full-year vendor count rather than the pilot count.

### Briefing for users

> We are selecting TraceDesk because the migration requires shared oversight of dependencies and contractual milestones across several vendors while preserving appropriate access boundaries. It is less immediately familiar than Boardly and will require more onboarding, but it should reduce recurring reconciliation work and provide a more reliable shared history. The pilot will verify access control, reporting, dependency management, usability, and full-scale cost before rollout.

If TraceDesk fails the permissions, reporting, adoption, or cost thresholds in that realistic test, Boardly is the sensible fallback. Otherwise, the evidence supports accepting TraceDesk’s higher initial effort in exchange for the workflow control you are likely to need throughout the year.

**Choose LocalMinutes**, assuming your organization can support the required local computing environment.

The deciding factor is the handling of sensitive participant information: the independent evaluation found that LocalMinutes processes audio on organization-controlled hardware and passed a deletion audit, whereas ClearScribe’s standard workflow uploads recordings to a shared cloud region. For clinical research interviews, that recurring data-governance advantage outweighs ClearScribe’s familiar, faster editor and easier introduction.

**Main caveat:** LocalMinutes will impose more operational friction. Its setup requires local resources, its transcript formatting is basic, and users may spend more time cleaning up transcripts. The implementation note also suggests this burden became more noticeable over several weeks than during the initial demonstration.

Use the two-week pilot to validate the recommendation, but do not treat a smooth small pilot as proof that the workflow will scale. Track:

- setup and support time;
- transcription accuracy on representative accents, terminology, and audio quality;
- cleanup minutes per recorded hour;
- local processing time and capacity;
- deletion, access-control, and audit behavior; and
- user errors or workarounds involving sensitive files.

The briefing can be succinct: **LocalMinutes is being selected for stronger control over participant data, not because it is the easiest editor.** ClearScribe remains the sensible fallback if your privacy review explicitly approves its cloud workflow and the sustained productivity cost of LocalMinutes proves unacceptable.

I would choose **AccentNote**.

The deciding requirement is reliable transcription when participants frequently switch between English and Spanish. The recent independent evaluation found **substantially fewer speaker-attribution and language-boundary errors** with AccentNote, whereas ClearScribe sometimes attached Spanish phrases to the wrong speaker. That is a recurring problem in your core workflow, so over a year it is likely to outweigh ClearScribe’s one-time advantage in familiarity and adoption.

**Main tradeoff:** AccentNote will be less convenient operationally. Expect a somewhat harder introduction, slower processing, and more limited export options. You also give up ClearScribe’s familiar editor and faster cleanup for ordinary English-only meetings.

For the two-week pilot, treat the small sample as a workflow check rather than proof of annual performance. Include the most code-switch-heavy sessions available and track:

- corrections to speaker attribution;
- corrections at English–Spanish transitions;
- total cleanup time per recording;
- export or integration friction;
- processing delays and user onboarding effort.

Brief users that the choice prioritizes **fewer recurring bilingual transcription errors over immediate familiarity and speed**. Set explicit acceptance thresholds before the pilot; otherwise, ClearScribe’s familiarity may feel disproportionately persuasive in such a short trial.

## Recommendation: adopt **Verbatim Cloud**

For this use case, its decisive advantage is **auditability**. The independent evaluation found that Verbatim Cloud:

- links each transcript sentence back to the corresponding audio; and
- separately identifies motions, amendments, and vote counts.

Those capabilities directly support your central requirement: being able to verify the exact wording and disposition of board actions. By contrast, ClearScribe’s condensed transcript missed two qualifications in the independent test. Even if those omissions are uncommon, they create the wrong kind of risk when a motion’s precise wording or vote must later be defended.

### Why it outweighs ClearScribe’s advantages

ClearScribe remains the easier and more familiar option. Its editor produces readable prose, requires less manual formatting, and would probably let you become productive faster. Those are meaningful benefits, not superficial preferences.

However, they primarily reduce a **one-time transition cost** and routine editing effort. Verbatim Cloud’s sentence-level audio links and structured action fields improve the **recurring verification workflow** for every consequential meeting. As annual volume grows, that difference is more likely to matter than it did in a small initial trial.

The main concession is that Verbatim Cloud produces a denser transcript and reviewers need time to confirm its structured fields. Adoption will therefore require more training and review discipline.

## How to use the two-week pilot

Treat the pilot as a **risk check and workflow-design exercise**, not simply a popularity contest or proof that the tool can transcribe meetings. A small sample may understate both recurring benefits and infrequent but serious errors.

Include meetings or recordings containing:

- formally worded motions and amendments;
- corrections, qualifications, or interruptions;
- multiple speakers with similar voices;
- abstentions, recusals, split votes, and roll-call votes;
- poor audio, overlapping speech, and remote participants.

Have reviewers compare both the transcript and draft minutes with the source audio. Track:

1. accuracy of exact motion and amendment wording;
2. accuracy of mover, seconder, and vote count;
3. omitted qualifications or conditions;
4. time required to verify each board action;
5. time required to produce final minutes;
6. errors that would have survived ordinary review;
7. reviewer confidence and training difficulties.

Set acceptance thresholds in advance—for example, **no untraceable motion or vote and no material error in final minutes**. Do not let faster cleanup compensate for an unverifiable board action.

## Briefing for users

Tell the team:

> We selected Verbatim Cloud because board actions must be traceable to the recording, not because it necessarily produces the prettiest first draft. Its motion, amendment, and vote fields are review aids rather than authoritative records. Reviewers must confirm each one against the linked audio before minutes are finalized.

Also establish:

- who verifies motions and votes;
- how corrections are documented;
- when the original recording may be deleted;
- who can access recordings and transcripts;
- how confidential or executive-session material is handled; and
- what happens if the tool cannot confidently identify a speaker or vote.

**Bottom line:** choose Verbatim Cloud and accept the additional learning and review effort. ClearScribe would be reasonable if speed and familiarity were the primary goals, but the evidence favors Verbatim Cloud for the requirement that governs this decision: independently verifiable motions and votes.

I recommend **Verbatim Cloud** for the one-year commitment.

The deciding factor is your non-negotiable workflow: **a reviewer must be able to trace each decision or action item back to the exact discussion**. In the independent evaluation, Verbatim Cloud linked those items directly to the relevant audio segments. ClearScribe produced smoother notes and offers a familiar, faster editor, but reviewers could not reliably verify two disputed interpretations. For design reviews, that is not a minor quality difference—it affects whether the record can settle later questions about what was agreed and why.

The implementation evidence also suggests this advantage persists beyond the demo. The pilot team found that Verbatim Cloud’s linked passages added review time and produced denser minutes, yet the recurring traceability benefit became more significant over several weeks. That is exactly the kind of benefit likely to repay the one-time learning and migration effort during a full-year deployment.

The trade-off is real: by choosing Verbatim Cloud, you give up ClearScribe’s familiarity, quick cleanup, and easier introduction. I would accept that cost because those strengths optimize routine note production, while Verbatim Cloud better protects the specific high-consequence task you identified: **defensible decisions tied to source evidence**.

A practical adoption approach would be to:

- standardize how decisions and action items are marked;
- train reviewers to validate only consequential or disputed items rather than every sentence;
- preserve audio links when exporting or archiving minutes;
- review after roughly 90 days whether verification time is manageable.

If traceability later proves rarely used, ClearScribe would be the sensible alternative at renewal. Based on the current requirement, however, **Verbatim Cloud is the more defensible choice despite its higher workflow cost**.

**Choose VaultBridge** for this workflow.

The decisive requirement is not ordinary password retrieval; it is granting contractors **time-limited database access without exposing standing credentials**. In the recent independent evaluation, VaultBridge provided expiring access, retained approval records, and automatically removed access at contract end. KeyNest required shared-vault membership and manual revocation. That recurring control advantage is likely to outweigh KeyNest’s familiar interface and lower migration effort once contractor volume increases.

**Main caveat:** VaultBridge will be harder and more expensive to introduce. Its contractor workflow costs extra, and managers must define access templates. A two-week, small-sample pilot may therefore overemphasize setup friction while understating the later benefit of automated expiry—or fail to reveal problems that appear only at higher volume.

